@meta
id: 11-14
title: Skip the ones that stay
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: cgn, dot-design
par: 34
minutes: 7
vscode: replace all, then go back and undo the two it ruined
open: money.ts

@brief
`pence` is being renamed to `minorUnits` across the service. In this file
the word appears eight times, and two of those are not the variable — one
is a comment written in English and one is a sentence shown to a customer.
Both of those say `pence` because that is the English word for it, and
both must stay exactly as they are.

Rename the six that are code. Leave the two that are prose.

A replace-all would take all eight and hand the customer a message about
minorUnits. This is the case that justifies changing matches one at a
time: you look at each one before you commit, and when a match is not
meant to change you step over it instead of changing it.

@keys
| `*` | search for the word under the cursor |
| `cgn` | change this match, or the next one if you are not on one |
| `.` | change the next match |
| `n` | step over a match without touching it |
| `u` | undo the one you should not have changed |

@hints
The keys are the same as the last lesson. What is new is that you are
choosing, match by match, between two of them.
--
`.` means "change the next match". `n` means "leave this one and show me
the next". Pressing `n` does not disturb the dot, so `.` still works
afterwards.
--
Land on the first `pence`, `*`, and read what it puts you on — the comment
on line six. `n` past it, change the next one, then dot your way down,
skipping the string with `n` when you reach it and remembering the match
you started on is still waiting for you after the wrap.

@solution
    jw  *  n  cgn minorUnits <Esc>  . . . .  n  n  .

Read that as a sentence. `*` picks up the word and lands on the comment;
`n` steps over it; `cgn` renames the function parameter; four dots take
the four matches after it; `n` steps over the customer-facing string; `n`
wraps to the top, back to the field declaration you started on; `.`
renames it.

Twelve decisions in the same number of keystrokes as a replace-all, and
none of them blind.

There is a real habit in here. Search-and-replace across a file asks you
to be certain up front, which on code you did not write is a thing you
cannot be. `cgn` with `.` and `n` inverts it: you are certain one match at
a time, and each match is its own undo step, so being wrong costs a single
`u` rather than a rethink of the whole file.

The tell that you are on the wrong tool is hesitating before pressing
Enter on a replace-all. If you are hesitating, the pattern is not tight
enough to be trusted, and you should be stepping instead.

@start money.ts
export type Money = {
  pence: number;
  currency: string;
};

// gateway amounts always arrive in pence
export function formatMoney(pence: number) {
  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;
}

export function refundSummary(order: Order) {
  const pence = order.total;

  return {
    pence,
    label: `We refunded ${formatMoney(pence)} to your card.`,
    note: 'Refunds are calculated in pence and rounded down.',
  };
}

@expected money.ts
export type Money = {
  minorUnits: number;
  currency: string;
};

// gateway amounts always arrive in pence
export function formatMoney(minorUnits: number) {
  return `£${(minorUnits / 100).toFixed(2)}`;
}

export function refundSummary(order: Order) {
  const minorUnits = order.total;

  return {
    minorUnits,
    label: `We refunded ${formatMoney(minorUnits)} to your card.`,
    note: 'Refunds are calculated in pence and rounded down.',
  };
}
